---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 6: CAD Leveling-Up II &mdash; A Full Practice Assembly</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 2</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 6</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">CAD Leveling-Up II</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Panels Was the Easy Case</h2>
              <p>One corner, one mate, easy to check by eye.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A real cabinet has several panels meeting at several corners
                &mdash; today, scale up.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Today's Build: An Open-Top Box</h2>
              <p>Front, back, two sides, and a bottom &mdash; five panels, four corner joints.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Same Skill, More of It</h2>
              <p>
                Every joint uses the exact same Fastened-mate technique from
                Session 5. Nothing new to learn &mdash; just more places for
                a small error to hide.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Name Your Parts</h2>
              <p>
                "Part 3" tells you nothing three steps later. "Front Panel,"
                "Left Side" does.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>One More Idea: Flat Panels Are What CAM Sees</h2>
              <p>
                Each panel is modeled as a 3D part, but for a flat panel,
                what the CAM software actually needs is the 2D outline.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>One More Idea: Flat Panels Are What CAM Sees</h2>
              <p>
                Thickness matters for the assembly fitting together.{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Shape</b> is what next week's CAM
                work is actually about.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">More parts means more places to check, not a harder technique.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                If one joint fails, isolate it. Don't assume the whole
                assembly is wrong.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A clean-looking assembly can still hide a bad joint.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Zoom into every corner individually &mdash; don't trust a
                glance at the whole box.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 7: Toolpath Types &amp; Profile Offset</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Your model leaves OnShape for{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Autodesk Fusion</b>, where a flat
                panel's shape becomes an actual cutting path.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Model Five Panels</h3>
          <p>
            Front, back, two sides, and a bottom &mdash; all at the same
            real stock thickness from Session 5. Name each part clearly
            (e.g., "Front Panel," "Left Side," "Bottom").
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Assemble and Mate All Four Corners</h3>
          <p>
            Using Fastened mates, build an open-top box. Every corner should
            use the same technique from Session 5 &mdash; just applied four
            times instead of once.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Verify Every Joint Individually</h3>
          <p>
            Zoom into each of the four corners separately. A box that
            "looks right" from a distance can still have one bad joint
            hiding in it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Note Any Fix You Had to Make</h3>
          <p>
            If any joint needed correcting, write down what was wrong and
            what you changed.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, confirm:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>All five panels are clearly named, not left as default part names</li>
            <li>All four corner joints are individually verified &mdash; no gaps, no overlaps</li>
            <li>Every joint uses a real Fastened mate</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>What's the difference between a panel's <i>thickness</i> mattering and a panel's <i>shape</i> mattering &mdash; and where does each one actually get used?</li>
            <li>Session 7 starts CAM, the software that turns these flat panels into toolpaths. Which of your five panels do you think will be hardest to cut, and what about its shape makes you say so?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your five-panel box assembly and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
